================================================================================
  Part 2: m-averaging and renormalization
================================================================================

  What you were handed
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

For each 3-day date range, Stanford gives us one power spectrum per degree l,
from 0 to 1000. Inside the file for degree l are the 2l+1 spectra belonging to
the individual azimuthal orders m. At l = 200 that is 401 separate spectra of
what is physically the same set of modes.

Those 2l+1 have names worth knowing, because they turn up in filenames and in
conversation. The m = 0 spectrum is zonal, its pattern is bands of latitude with
no variation going around. The |m| = l ones are sectoral, sliced like an orange
from pole to pole. Everything in between is tesseral, a checkerboard.

They are separate because the Sun rotates. A mode running with the rotation
comes back Doppler-shifted up, one running against it comes back shifted down.
What would be a single line on a non-rotating star is instead smeared across a
band by rotational splitting, and the smear is widest at high l where there are
the most m values to spread out.

Three days is not much data. Any one of those 2l+1 spectra on its own is mostly
noise. Averaged together they become a peak. But you cannot simply add them,
because you would be adding a band and not a line.

  What m-averaging does
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Undo the smear first, then add. Three steps.

  1. Work out the shift. Within a multiplet, meaning all the modes sharing one n
     and one l, the way frequency depends on m is captured by a short
     polynomial:

       nu(n,l,m) = nu(n,l) + L * sum_k a_k(n,l) * P_k(m/L),    L^2 = l(l+1)

     The a_k are the splitting coefficients and the P_k are Legendre
     polynomials. Odd k comes from rotation. Even k comes from asphericity, the
     Sun not being quite round, and from magnetic fields. The coefficients are
     measured by cross-correlating the spectra against each other, and here we
     mostly use the three lowest odd ones: a1, a3, and a5.

  2. Slide each spectrum back by the amount step 1 says rotation moved it.

  3. Add them up. The shifted tesseral and sectoral spectra plus the unshifted
     zonal one, combined into a single spectrum for that degree. The combination
     is weighted, using the inverse of each spectrum's mean power between 1500
     and 4500 microhertz, so that the noisier m values count for less.

Out comes one clean spectrum per degree. Those are the Y*aspc2nonav* files, and
there should be 1001 of them.

  Two details that would otherwise look arbitrary
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Why l = 0 needs its own script.
  l = 0 has 2(0)+1 = 1 azimuthal order. There is nothing to average, so the
  m-averaging machinery skips it and you end up with 1000 files instead of 1001.
  cp000file.sh puts the l = 0 spectrum in by hand. If your count comes back
  1000, this is nearly always the reason.

Why renormalization is a separate pass.
  A power spectrum is not only the modes. It is the modes convolved with the
  window function of the observing run, because the time series is finite and is
  never perfectly gapless. That convolution changes how much total power lands
  in the spectrum, by an amount that depends on the particular run. Two date
  ranges with different duty cycles would not otherwise be comparable, and a
  3-day spectrum and a 72-day one certainly would not be.

  Renormalization divides that out. The number it divides by is the window sum,
  and it arrives from the pwin step: the gap record for the run is pulled from
  JSOC, its power spectrum is computed, and the values are written into the psdr
  scripts before you ever run them. Out come the Y*cspc2nonav* files, again 1001
  of them, each the same length as the Y*aspc2nonav* file of matching degree.

  It is worth opening pwin.out for your run and looking at the numbers. Most
  3-day ranges sit within a fraction of a percent of unity, which makes it easy
  to assume the step does nothing. It is not always that way. A range that lost
  data can carry a correction approaching ten percent, and ten percent of the
  power is not a rounding error.

  One thing renormalization does not fix: the fact that we only ever see one
  apodized hemisphere. That is the spatial side of the same story, it shows up
  as leakage between neighbouring degrees rather than as a scale error, and
  Part 3 is where it gets dealt with.

  Why it matters downstream
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Everything Part 3 measures, it measures on your output. A peak that is still
smeared reads as a wider peak, and a wider peak reads as a larger linewidth.
Since Gamma is the quantity this group is after, a sloppy m-average does not
merely add noise. It adds a bias shaped exactly like signal.

